# Line endings.
#
# The repository is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which left the
# working tree with a LF/CRLF mixture: 11 of the 28 packaged files were LF on disk
# and 17 were CRLF, purely depending on whether git or an editor last wrote each
# one. The stored blobs have always been LF. Declaring it here makes the working
# tree agree with the blobs instead of depending on a global user setting.
#
# Note for anyone comparing a release archive against this repository: the code
# handoff archives are built from `git cat-file blob`, so they are byte-identical
# to HEAD and uniformly LF. Comparing one against a CRLF working tree will show
# spurious differences.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly text, LF.
*.py     text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.toml   text eol=lf
*.cfg    text eol=lf
*.ini    text eol=lf
*.sh     text eol=lf

# Binary, never converted or diffed as text.
*.zip    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.docx   binary
*.npy    binary
*.npz    binary
*.pt     binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.bundle binary

# Score matrices and submission members are byte-pinned artifacts. Their line
# endings are part of the accepted result -- dataset1.csv is CRLF and dataset2.csv
# is LF, each in the exact form that was scored online -- so they must never be
# normalised. They are gitignored today; this rule is a guard in case one is ever
# added deliberately.
*.csv    -text
